sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch13-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A natural arrangement of groups must be strictly genealogical, but branches that are equally closely related to a common progenitor may differ greatly in how much they have been modified, and these differing amounts of difference are what is expressed by ranking them as different genera, families or orders.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Branches equally related by descent can differ greatly in amount of modification";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XIII, “Mutual Affinities of Organic Beings: Morphology: Embryology: Rudimentary Organs”; section “Rules and difficulties in classification, explained on the theory of descent with modification”. Scope: all classes of organisms"
}
